Modifications
-------------
Operon's fork discipline is that upstream is changed as little as possible: the
whole point of building on Kaneo is to keep Kaneo's feature set — boards,
backlog, labels, time tracking, webhooks, search and the MCP server — exactly as
upstream built it. Only eight kinds of change are permitted, and every file this
fork touches is declared in Operon's `docs/fork-discipline.md`:

  1. Auth wiring — Operon is the OpenID Connect provider; Kaneo's own local
     sign-in is disabled by environment, and the post-login hook reports the new
     Kaneo user back to Operon.
  2. Branding and chrome — the product is called Initiative inside Operon, and
     the sidebar carries Operon's surface switcher.
  3. A `telegraph` integration type with an authorised external-link write
     route, so a Kaneo task can carry the Nostr event id of the chat message it
     came from.
  4. Workspace bootstrap — a single shared workspace, created by the first
     admin's login and joined by everyone after.
  5. Build hygiene — one `.dockerignore` line keeping a build-cache directory
     out of the image's context.
  6. Workspace permissions in Operon mode — an added member payload that lets
     members assign and delete tasks; upstream's own `member` payload is
     untouched, so an instance that is not Operon's behaves as it always did.
  7. Object storage — the presigned upload URL is rewritten onto an origin a
     browser outside the compose network can actually reach, and only when the
     deployment opts in.
  8. Egress policy — a private-destination allowlist for outbound webhook and
     Gitea targets, so a production deployment can permit exactly the internal
     receiver Operon runs instead of switching every private range back on with
     upstream's `KANEO_ALLOW_PRIVATE_WEBHOOK_DESTINATIONS` boolean.

That count is RE-DERIVED from this branch's tree whenever it changes, never
incremented from a previous reading: `docs/fork-discipline.md` §3 carries the
touch list the eight kinds map onto, path by path, and §7 records which task
landed each one. An inventory whose number nobody re-checked is what makes a
fork's provenance untrustworthy.

A fork commit that touches any path not declared in `docs/fork-discipline.md`
is a defect, not a feature: the touch list is what keeps this fork mergeable
with upstream.
